## Why This Is Asked

Tests knowledge of NUMA-aware memory management and lock-free structures beyond simple queues. Candidates must justify locality decisions, reclamation delay trade-offs, and fragmentation management under mixed workloads. The prompt also probes API design, minimal synchronization strategies, and practical benchmarking methodology.

## Key Concepts

- **NUMA locality**: Allocations should prefer the local node to avoid expensive remote memory access
- **Slab allocator design**: Pre-allocates memory in fixed-size slabs per object size class
- **Size bucketing**: Groups objects into size classes (32–128B, 1–4KB) to reduce fragmentation
- **Lock-free free lists**: CAS-based singly-linked lists enable concurrent access without heavy locking
- **Cross-node reclamation**: Deferred reclamation via epoch orquEUe avoids false sharing and reduces ping-ponging
- **Alignment and false sharing**: Cache-line padding prevents unrelated objects from sharing the same line
- **TLB/cache effects**: Slab locality improves cache hit rates; large pages (huge TLB entries) reduce TLB misses for bulk operations

## Code Example

```c
#include <stdint.h>
#include <stdbool.h>

typedef enum { SMALL_32B, SMALL_64B, SMALL_128B, MEDIUM_1KB, MEDIUM_4KB, NUM_BUCKETS } bucket_t;

typedef struct free_node {
    struct free_node *next;
} free_node_t;

typedef struct {
    free_node_t *head;
    _Atomic uint64_t epoch;
    char pad[64 - sizeof(void*) - sizeof(_Atomic uint64_t)];
} alignas(64) lockfree_freelist_t;

typedef struct {
    lockfree_freelist_t freelists[NUM_BUCKETS];
    void *slab_start;
    size_t slab_size;
    int node_id;
    bool preallocate;
} alignas(64) numa_slab_cache_t;

typedef struct {
    numa_slab_cache_t *caches;
    int num_nodes;
    size_t page_size;
    void *(*numa_alloc)(size_t size, int node);
    void (*numa_free)(void *ptr, size_t size);
} numa_allocator_t;

numa_allocator_t *numa_allocator_create(int num_nodes, void *(*alloc_fn)(size_t, int), void (*free_fn)(void*, size_t));
void *numa_alloc(numa_allocator_t *alloc, size_t size, int node_hint);
void numa_free(numa_allocator_t *alloc, void *ptr, size_t size, int target_node);
void numa_allocator_destroy(numa_allocator_t *alloc);
```

## Fragmentation Considerations

- **Internal fragmentation**: Inherent in fixed-size bucketing; acceptable given latency requirements
- **External fragmentation**: Mitigated by pre-allocated slabs and lazy reclamation (freed objects held until next allocation)
- **Cross-node fragmentation**: Remote frees accumulate in target node queues; monitored via periodic compaction

## TLB/Cache Effects

- Slab allocation ensures objects within a slab share TLB entries
- Cache-line-aligned objects prevent false sharing in multiprocess workloads
- Large pages (2MB/1GB) recommended for bulk allocations to reduce TLB pressure
- NUMA interleaving disabled for hot paths; manual placement preferred

